playable.evidence:
The rules cover the following:
- card choice;
- forced season end on the 4th card, with that card unowned;
- simultaneous talk, then a simultaneous sealed bid;
- winner, tie-break and an all-zero rule;
- who receives payment, and that the auctioneer pays the bank on their own card;
- ranking tie-breaks;
- payouts, and the third artist paying nothing despite earlier value;
- resets;
- auctioneer passing, both at step 10 and at season end (e);
- the game ending after season 3.

Finiteness. Each season plays at most 3+3+3+1 = 10 cards, so there are at most 30 turns and 30 rounds or fewer. Each seat plays at most 8 of its 9 cards, and Amber needs at most 12 of its 14 cards, so no supply runs out.

Limits. Steps 18 <= 20. Choice steps 3 <= 4 (card, talk, bid). Attributes 19 <= 20: per player money, hand, and owned A/B/C; global values x3, counts x3, season, auctioneer, current card, high bid, winner. Hidden elements 2 <= 3. Random draws: the shuffle happens once at setup, 0 per round. Rounds 30 <= 40.

Engine fit.
- The ranking is cheap. First place is simply the artist of the current card. Second place is one comparison between the other two artists with the rarer-wins tie-break.
- The winner and seat-order tie-break is a single calculation.
- Season-end steps and post-game rounds can carry arithmetic `when` conditions. No block-branching is needed.
- No board, clock, trading or binding agreements are used.

Talk is justified. Players disagree, because the auctioneer wants high bids and bidders want low ones. Bidders may try a non-binding low-bid ring that can be betrayed in the sealed bid. Players also have motives to push or bluff which artists they will feed. Talk is simultaneous.

This is not a repeated dilemma in which a safe option wins late rounds.
